{"id": "0394a098-84fe-41b1-8190-b589c71af509", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T04:43:42.650910", "source": "user", "parent_id": "51cb29ca-1172-47c3-92b9-843b531c7f76", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"We are at the initial_inspection phase for a design-only task. Please do NOT yet produce the full final experiment design. Instead, complete the following three items and return them concisely in one response (Markdown sections are fine). No experiment execution is required or expected at this stage, and do not fabricate empirical results or reference a paper we have not placed in the project context. Treat any specific claim about CAMUV internals as an assumption unless you can ground it in code or docs that are visible to you in /workspace or elsewhere on the filesystem; otherwise flag it as assumed and explain why it is plausible enough to plan around. Our submission contract is a final experiment design (rationale, controls, analysis plan, feasibility) — empirical execution is explicitly out of scope. The runtime is a bubblewrap sandbox with network disabled, system Python only, and no preinstalled project dependencies; GPU is off and CPU affinity is 24. There are no provided datasets. So when you propose an approach, account for the fact that any real-data experiments would need either synthetic data we generate ourselves, or a dataset that is already part of the repository. We do not need this in your first reply, but eventually the design must include at least one condition that varies the presence/strength of unobserved confounding so the claim that CAMUV can discover causal relationships under latent common causes is actually exercised in a controlled way. With all of that in mind, please address these three points and stop there. 1) Input / repository inspection: report what is actually present at /workspace (files, directories, any code, any docs, any datasets, any existing CAMUV implementation). If relevant material exists, summarize what it gives us; if nothing exists, say so explicitly rather than inventing one. Do not install packages, fetch from the network, or run heavy computations — keep this a lightweight inspection only. 2) Proposed approach: outline the experiment design you would produce to answer the project question — how CAMUV would be used to discover causal relationships in data with unobserved common causes, what synthetic data generation scheme you would use (graph family, choice of latent vs observed variables, sample sizes, distributions of noise/mechanisms, intervention vs observational regimes), what baselines you would compare against and why these controls matter, what metrics you would report (e.g., structural Hamming distance, F1 on recovered edges/shell, orientation accuracy), what ablation axes are most informative (number of latent confounders, latent strength, sample size, graph density), and how the analysis distinguishes true recovery from spurious edges caused by latent confounding. 3) Assumptions and uncertainties: list every consequential assumption you are leaning on — including any assumptions about how CAMUV works and what its inputs/outputs are, what a reasonable synthetic SCM looks like, independence/distributional assumptions, what is and is not identifiable, choice of hyperparameters, and any unknowns in the resource/environment that could affect feasibility. For each, state whether it is supported by something visible in the repo, derived from general causal-discovery knowledge, or an open guess to be resolved at approach_review. Keep the whole reply compact — a few hundred lines at most. After I review this I will either approve the approach or send a focused clarification.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"How can you discover causal relationships in data with unobserved common causes using the CAMUV algorithm?\", \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\", \"environment\": {\"runtime\": \"bubblewrap\", \"network\": \"disabled\", \"repository\": \"/workspace\", \"python\": \"python3\", \"software_environment\": \"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\", \"datasets\": [], \"gpu_enabled\": false, \"cpu_affinity_count\": 24, \"note\": \"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\"}, \"researcher_topics\": [], \"question_budget\": 6, \"actor_step_budget\": 60, \"continue_after_initial_plan\": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 1}
