{"id": "3cd2228d-349f-4451-a0e2-250d3b4af438", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T04:46:33.224993", "source": "user", "parent_id": "3b2ee63b-3fa3-4ae5-8352-f8029057fbe6", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Thanks — the inspection is honest, the proposal is grounded, and I am approving it. Resolutions to your eight uncertainties so they don't leak into the final design as open items, followed by a short list of additions to fold in as you write the deliverable. Treat everything below as binding for the final design unless you spot a direct contradiction with a visible file (you've already confirmed there are none for CAMUV internals, so defaults stand and must be marked Assumed where I say so). (U1) Output object. CAMUV's output type is not visible; assume it returns an augmented CPDAG on observed variables with per-pair latent-shell scores and (optionally) latent-graph labels. Scoring will be lenient: evaluate observable F1_adj, F1_skel, SHD, orientation accuracy all on this returned graph (CPDAG projection, untangle where possible), evaluate F1_shell by thresholding the per-pair latent score into latent memberships and comparing the unordered child-sets to the true observed children of each ground-truth latent. Mark the entire object contract as Assumed; if F1_shell is undefined because CAMUV produces only pairwise scores without shell labels, define shell membership by single-linkage clustering of the pairwise score above a documented threshold τ and include a sensitivity row for τ∈{0.3,0.5,0.7}. (U2) Intervention indicator. Assume CAMUV accepts a per-row intervention indicator (the do() flag), consistent with modern latent-aware methods. Include |Targets|∈{0,3,6} cells as part of the design; |Targets|=0 is the observational control. Document the per-row indicator contract as Assumed and add a one-line note: if not supported at execution, the intervention cells collapse to observational and are reported as such. (U3) Latent recovery. Score both observable recovery AND shell recovery; do NOT score full latent-graph F1 unless CAMUV returns a discrete latent node set. (U4) Scaling. Keep the (p,k_lat) envelope at p∈{8,15,30}, k_lat∈{1,2,3} (treat k_lat=0 as the no-latent control baseline; do not charge it against CAMUV's value claim). Add an explicit 'small' envelope p∈{8}, n∈{500,2000}, k_lat∈{1,2} as the documented feasibility branch under stdlib-only compute; the small envelope is the minimum evidence bar, not the headline result, and the design must say so. (U5) Placeholder callable. Yes — treat CAMUV as a documented external callable with the assumed signature; the substantive content of the design is SCM scheme + baselines + metrics + analysis plan, and that must be true independent of any specific implementation. State this explicitly in the design's 'Method contract' subsection. (U6) Hyperparameters. Adopt your own sweep as the design default: λ∈{1e-3,1e-2,1e-1} and k_max∈{1,2,3}; lock the headline result to the median-best configuration; report sensitivity in a secondary figure. Document this as the design's chosen default; a researcher-supplied override is a downstream substitution. (U7) Shell threshold. Default τ=0.5 with sensitivity at {0.3,0.7}; document in the metrics subsection. (U8) Identifiability. Include two negative-control cells — two latents with ≥2 overlapping observed children, and a single latent with full mediation (A→L→B, L observed only through B) — and label them explicitly as 'unidentifiable / not evidential' in the results table; they exist to demonstrate the design knows where the claim must stop, not to test against CAMUV. Additions to fold into the final design. (A) Add a 'Minimum evidence bar' subsection stating the conditions under which the question is answered positively. Concrete proposal: across the (p=8, n∈{500,2000}, k_lat=2, mechanism=nonlinear-additive, ξ=1.0) cell band, the design-positive answer is CAMUV F1_adj above the matched-sample FCI baseline by ≥0.10 absolute AND CSR (defined below) below FCI's CSR by ≥0.10 absolute; thresholds are illustrative and must be stated as the design's choice. (B) Sharpen the operational definition of the confounding-spurious-edge rate (CSR). Define 'extra edge' as any directed edge (i,j) returned in the algorithm's observable CPDAG that is not in any I-equivalent skeleton of the latent-augmented true DAG when projected to observables (banned-edges set computed from true DAG via d-separation). Then CSR = |{extra edges (i,j) : ∃ latent L with L→i and L→j in true augmented DAG}| / |{extra edges}|. Report both the fraction and the absolute count per replicate; include the rate-per-shell (CSR / |true shells|) as a third column. (C) Define one 'headline result' the design will report: a single primary figure with three panels — (i) F1_adj vs ξ (latent strength) at fixed (p=15, k_lat=2, n=2000, nonlinear-additive, |Targets|=3) for {PC, FCI, GES, CAMUV−latent, CAMUV}; (ii) CSR vs ξ on the same conditions; (iii) F1_shell vs n at ξ=1.0 for CAMUV only, with sensitivity band over τ. Everything else in the analysis plan is secondary. (D) Move the feasibility statement from assumptions into the analysis plan: name it 'Compute envelope & staged execution' and state (i) stdlib-only fallback executes the small envelope p≤8, n≤2000, k_lat≤2, ≤30 replicates per cell; (ii) full envelope requires numpy/scipy and the assumed CAMUV callable and is documented as the design's intended scope, with the small envelope being the minimum-evidence subset. (E) Add an 'Error-type diagnosis' subsection: for each method, plot a 2×2 confusion of (recovered ∩ true) vs (recovered ∖ true) edges, classified by 'shares a latent with the true neighbor' vs 'does not', so the design can attribute extra edges either to latent confounding (expected for PC/GES under latent load) or to other failure modes. (F) Add a 'What we do NOT claim' subsection listing: full latent-graph recovery F1, identifiability beyond the standard Markov/projection equivalence class for the SCM family used, performance outside the swept hyperparameter box, and behavior under non-additive / non-Gaussian mechanisms beyond the heteroskedastic class tested. These are negative results the design pre-commits to ignoring. Now produce the final experiment design as a single self-contained Markdown document with the following top-level structure, in this order, and stop: Title; 1. Question & claim scope (paraphrase of project question + the explicit 'do' / 'do NOT' list from F); 2. Method contract (Assumed CAMUV I/O from U1/U2/U5, hyperparameter policy from U6); 3. Data generating process (SCM family, axes, ranges, intervention semantics from U2, including the overlapping-latent and full-mediation negative controls from U8); 4. Baselines & controls (PC, FCI, GES/FGES, CAMUV−latent, random floor) with one-line rationale per baseline explaining what each contrast isolates; 5. Metrics (F1_adj, F1_skel, SHD, orientation accuracy, F1_shell with τ from U7, CSR from B, plus rate-per-shell); 6. Ablation axes & priority order (ξ first, then k_lat, then n, then mechanism class, then interventional dose, then p); 7. Minimum evidence bar (from A, including the F1_adj / CSR thresholds as illustrative); 8. Headline result figure specification (from C, three panels, fixed conditions); 9. Error-type diagnosis plan (from E); 10. Compute envelope & staged execution (from D); 11. Identifiability boundaries & negative controls (from U8 + the 'What we do NOT claim' list from F); 12. Open clarifications deferred to a future round (a short list of items still genuinely unresolved after this exchange; do not pad). Do not produce empirical numbers, do not claim any experiment was run, and keep the document tight — aim for ~400–700 lines of Markdown. Begin when ready.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 20}
{"id": "bf2fbfbc-1f7c-4048-b849-0e6de011138e", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T04:48:41.263784", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "3cd2228d-349f-4451-a0e2-250d3b4af438", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-c3145161-6195-4eff-a54d-44dc0fb577a7", "tool_call": {"id": "call-c3145161-6195-4eff-a54d-44dc0fb577a7", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Final experiment design for CAMUV on latent-confounded SCMs; full structure, no execution claimed.\",\"stage\":\"result\",\"answer\":\"# Final Experiment Design — Causal Discovery under Unobserved Common Causes with CAMUV\\n\\n*Design-only deliverable. No experiment was executed. All CAMUV-internal claims are marked **Assumed** and grounded in only that the workspace contained no implementation, paper, or docs (verified by inspection of `/workspace`).*\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 1. Question & Claim Scope\\n\\n**Project question.** *How can we discover causal relationships in data with unobserved common causes using the CAMUV algorithm?*\\n\\nThe question is decomposed into two operational claims this design attempts to support (within a defined envelope):\\n\\n- **C-Dir** — *Direction claim:* on data generated by a latent-augmented SCM (linear-Gaussian or nonlinear-additive) with at least one latent confounder, CAMUV's recovered observable graph has higher adjacency F1 and lower confounding-spurious-edge rate than constraint-based and score-based baselines that ignore latents, and is at least non-inferior to a generic latent-aware baseline (FCI).\\n- **C-Shell** — *Shell claim:* CAMUV's per-pair latent score, thresholded and clustered, recovers a non-trivial fraction of the observed-children sets (\\\"shells\\\") of true latent parents, where the identity of latents is not directly observable.\\n\\n### Claim scope — what the design DOES claim\\n\\n- Within the swept hyperparameter box (`λ ∈ {1e−3, 1e−2, 1e−1}`, `k_max ∈ {1, 2, 3}`) and the data envelope in §3, CAMUV's behaviour is exercised on synthetic SCMs with explicit unobserved confounders and is comparable, on defined metrics, to the baselines in §4.\\n- The headline figure (§8) is the primary visual evidence.\\n- The minimum evidence bar (§7) is the binary decision rule for the C-Dir claim within the small-envelope feasibility band.\\n\\n### Claim scope — what the design does NOT claim (from F)\\n\\n- **No full latent-graph F1** — full discovery of the latent-variable set identity is **not** scored unless CAMUV returns a discrete latent node set; otherwise the latent side is evaluated only as shell recovery.\\n- **No identifiability beyond the standard Markov / projection equivalence class** for the SCM family used. Equivalent DAGs that differ only within the equivalence class are treated as ground-truth variations, not as errors.\\n- **No claims outside the swept hyperparameter box.** Performance at `λ` or `k_max` values not in the sweep is out of scope.\\n- **No mechanism-class claims beyond `linear-Gaussian`, `nonlinear-additive`, and `heteroskedastic`.** Multiplicative-noise, post-nonlinear, heavy-tailed, or discrete mechanisms are not in scope.\\n- **No claim that CAMUV resolves fully-mediated latents.** A latent observable only through a single descendant (§11) is documented as unidentifiable and excluded from evidential cells.\\n- **No claim about runtime or scaling outside §10's compute envelope.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 2. Method Contract\\n\\n### 2.1 CAMUV — assumed external callable (Assumed throughout — see §12)\\n\\nThe design treats CAMUV as a documented external function with the following assumed signature and contract; the substantive content of this design (SCM scheme, baselines, metrics, analysis) is independent of any specific implementation.\\n\\n```\\ncamuv_fit(D, I, hyperparams) -> G_aug, S_lat\\n  D            : ℝ^{n×p}  — observed-variable data matrix (no latents)\\n  I            : {0,1}^n  or length-n list — per-row intervention indicator, 1 iff that row was drawn under do(X_t=…) for some target t\\n  hyperparams  : {lambda, k_max, tau, ...} — see §2.3\\n  G_aug        : CPDAG-on-observables augmented with latent-shell labels — Assumed\\n  S_lat[i,j]   : ℝ in [0,1]  — per-pair latent-shell score for observed pair (i,j) — Assumed\\n                  (higher score ⇒ larger posterior mass that i and j share a latent parent)\\n```\\n\\n**Subject to §1's \\\"do NOT claim\\\" list, the contract is sufficient for §5's metrics to be well-defined.** If an implementation differs, the design degrades as noted in §2.4.\\n\\n### 2.2 Intervention indicator (per U2 — Assumed)\\n\\n- Per-row boolean `I`. CAMUV is assumed to consume `I` and use it to disambiguate latent structure (consistent with modern latent-aware methods).\\n- Per-row: `I[t] = 1` means row is sampled after `do(X_t = a)` for one of the declared targets `t`. Rows not under any of the declared targets have `I = 0`.\\n- **Documented escape:** if a realised CAMUV implementation ignores `I` (or has no parameter for it), every interventional cell collapses to observational behaviour. The design records this event in §11's table and does not re-attribute the headline result.\\n\\n### 2.3 Hyperparameter policy (per U6)\\n\\n| Parameter | Default sweep | Locked configuration for headline | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| `λ` (regularisation) | `{1e−3, 1e−2, 1e−1}` | Median-best across the sweep at a fixed cell | If `λ` is undefined for a given CAMUV build, lock at `λ = 1e−2` and note in §12. |\\n| `k_max` (latent-cardinality cap) | `{1, 2, 3}` | Median-best across the sweep | If absent, set to `3` (envelope ceiling) and note in §12. |\\n| `τ` (shell threshold; not an algorithm hyper-parameter, but a metric-side policy) | default `τ = 0.5` with sensitivity `{0.3, 0.7}` in §5 | `τ = 0.5` for headline | See §5.3. |\\n\\nA researcher-supplied override is a downstream substitution; the design's default sweep is the binding choice unless explicitly amended.\\n\\n### 2.4 Latent-term ablation (per U1, U5)\\n\\n- `CAMUV −latent` = same callable with the latent-term contribution disabled (Assumed: implementation exposes a flag). This is in-family and differs from CAMUV only in the latent component.\\n- Fallback (Assumed): if the latent-term flag is unavailable, `CAMUV −latent` is replaced by an observational no-latent counterpart (PC by default) **sharing CAMUV's other hyperparameters where possible**, with the substitution explicitly recorded. The within-family isolation contrast is then downgraded from `(CAMUV vs CAMUV−latent)` to `(CAMUV vs shared-hyperparameter no-latent method)`.\\n\\n### 2.5 What the contract is NOT\\n\\n- The contract does not specify whether CAMUV returns CPDAG, PAG, MAG, or an \\\"augmented CPDAG\\\" — that ambiguity is folded into §5's scoring tolerance: we evaluate observable metrics against the observable projection under the assumption of CPDAG-style semantics, and \\\"untangle where possible\\\". Any deviation is documented but not changed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 3. Data Generating Process\\n\\nAll cells share the following generative backbone; axes vary only the bracketed factor.\\n\\n### 3.1 Top-level observable graph\\n\\n- Variable count `p` ∈ **{8, 15, 30}** (per \\\"headline\\\" envelope; per U4 the small-envelope branch uses only `p = 8`). The minimum-evidence bar is over the small envelope; the headline figure is at `p = 15`.\\n- Topology: Erdős–Rényi DAG with expected in-degree `d` ∈ **{1.0, 1.5, 3.0}**. Default `d = 1.5`.\\n- Edge coefficients `β_ji` (linear regime) sampled independently per edge from `Uniform([-1.05, -0.30] ∪ [0.30, 1.05])` so that coefficients are bounded away from zero and consistent with a generic identifiable SCM.\\n\\n### 3.2 Latent confounders\\n\\n- `k_lat` ∈ **{0, 1, 2, 3}** with `k_lat = 0` reserved as the no-latent control baseline (per U4, not an evidential cell for CAMUV's claim; it is reported only to anchor baselines).\\n- Each latent `U_ℓ` has `m_ℓ` ∈ **{2, 3, 4}** observed children, sampled i.i.d. uniformly without replacement from `V = {X_1, …, X_p}` excluding variables already fixed as latent parents of another latent at the same time step.\\n- **Latent strength axis** (the mandatory exercise of the latent-confounding claim): scale `ξ ∈ {0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0}` multiplies the latent-to-observed structural coefficients `γ_jℓ`. `ξ = 0.0` ⇒ latents exist in the truth but with zero influence (degenerate but informative); `ξ ∈ {1.0, 1.5, 2.0}` are the evidential cells.\\n- `ξ = 0` is reported as a robustness check (latent-aware methods should not over-attribute structure when latents are silent); it does not bear on the positive claim.\\n\\n### 3.3 Mechanism classes\\n\\n| Class | Functional form | Notes |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Linear-Gaussian** | `X_j = Σ_i β_ji X_i + ξ·Σ_ℓ γ_jℓ U_ℓ + η_j`, `η_j ~ 𝒩(0, σ_j²)` | Identifiable only up to Markov equivalence after projection. Baseline. |\\n| **Nonlinear-additive** | `X_j = Σ_i f_ji(X_i) + ξ·Σ_ℓ g_jℓ(U_ℓ) + η_j` | Each `f_ji`, `g_jℓ` drawn from a standardised cubic-spline basis with ≤3 dof, scaled so its marginal variance matches the linear regime's `β_ji² · Var(X_i)`. CAMUV's expected strong regime. |\\n| **Heteroskedastic** | Linear-Gaussian with `η_j ~ 𝒩(0, σ_j²·(1 + α·X_{pa(j)}²))`, small `α` | Tests robustness to mild heteroskedasticity. |\\n\\nNoise variances `σ_j²` are scaled so each variable has unit marginal variance before interventions.\\n\\n### 3.4 Sample size\\n\\n`n` ∈ **{500, 2000, 10000}**. Default for most cells: `n = 2000`.\\n\\n### 3.5 Interventions (per U2)\\n\\n- `|Targets|` ∈ **{0, 3, 6}**. `|Targets| = 0` is observational-only (the baseline). `|Targets| = 3`: do on a small fixed-size subset randomly chosen with at most one per latent's neighbourhood. `|Targets| = 6`: analogous, with two per latent-shell area where applicable.\\n- For each target `X_t` under intervention, the structural equation of `X_t` is replaced by an independent draw from the same mechanism family with a fresh noise variance; descendants update accordingly (interventional truncation in the latent-augmented DAG is preserved).\\n- Per-row indicator `I` is `1` for rows drawn under any declared `do`, else `0`. Propagated to CAMUV.\\n- **Documented failure mode** (U2 note): If CAMUV does not consume `I`, every interventional cell collapses to observational behaviour; we record the cell as \\\"(do() ignored)\\\" rather than inferring a latent-aware intervention effect.\\n\\n### 3.6 Replicates & randomisation\\n\\n- **30 random graph topologies × 30 mechanism seeds** per cell ⇒ **900 replicates per cell**.\\n- Per-replicate seed is the integer `seed(graph_id, mechanism_id)` of fixed stride, so that cells sharing `graph_id` use the same topology.\\n\\n### 3.7 Negative-control configurations (per U8)\\n\\nTwo purpose-built configurations are added **outside the evidential table** and labelled *unidentifiable / not evidential*. They exist to bound where the claim must stop.\\n\\n| Control | Construction | Why it is non-evidential |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **NC-OL** — overlapping latents | `k_lat = 2`, both share at least 2 observed children (`V_{U1} ∩ V_{U2} ≥ 2`). | Marginal-likelihood equivalence classes can swap the latent identities; shell F1 is identity-sensitive. |\\n| **NC-FM** — fully-mediated latent | Single latent `L → B` with `A → L → B`, where `B` is the only observed child. | With one observed child, shell size = 1; CAMUV has no signal that distinguishes `L` from a direct edge `A → B`. |\\n\\nNC cells are reported in a separate \\\"Identifiability boundaries\\\" table (§11); they never enter the headline or minimum-evidence bar.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Baselines & Controls\\n\\nEach contrast isolates a single factor; the design exploits this to attribute effects to specific algorithmic components.\\n\\n| Baseline | Type | Contrast vs CAMUV | Rationale — what this isolates |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| **PC** | Constraint-based, observational, no-latent | CAMUV's contribution over CI testing alone | CI-test noise vs. modelling noise; a huge PC deficit under `ξ > 0` is attributable to latent confounding, not to CI choice. |\\n| **FCI** (Lil columns: observational; Lips: with-intervention) | Constraint-based, latent-aware | CAMUV's contribution over generic latent-aware constraint search | Whether CAMUV's specific machinery (shell score + score-side latent modelling) adds value over a well-engineered PAG algorithm. |\\n| **GES / FGES** | Score-based, observational, no-latent | CAMUV's contribution over observational score-based discovery | Whether a no-latent score method does well enough to make the latent-aware machinery redundant under the swept envelope. |\\n| **CAMUV −latent** | In-family ablation of CAMUV | CAMUV's specific latent term | The strongest within-family control: isolates the marginal value of CAMUV's latent component under otherwise-identical hyperparameters. |\\n| **`Random`** | Uniform random orientation / skeleton | Floor | Sanity floor on orientation and shell metrics. |\\n\\nPC and GES are the \\\"no-latent\\\" baselines; FCI is the \\\"latent-aware but generic\\\" baseline; CAMUV −latent is the \\\"in-family no-latent\\\" baseline. Any headline gap for CAMUV should be visible against all three; otherwise the design attributes the gap to something else (e.g., CI-test choice if PC-equivalent, scoring choice if GES-equivalent).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 5. Metrics\\n\\nFor every reproducible combination of `(method, cell, replicate)`, the metrics below are computed against the observable projection of the latent-augmented truth. Where directions are involved, scoring is tolerant of CPDAG/PAG ambiguity per §2.5.\\n\\n### 5.1 Observable-graph metrics (against returned CPDAG-style projection)\\n\\n- **F1_adj** — F1 over unordered observable pairs for \\\"any kind of connection\\\" (`X_i — X_j` in any direction or undirected edge, vs. absent). Captures both missed and false connections.\\n- **F1_skel** — F1 over undirected skeleton edges only, ignoring orientation. Decouples skeleton recovery from orientation correctness.\\n- **SHD** — Structural Hamming Distance between recovered and true DAG-on-observables, accounting for missing, extra, and mis-oriented edges. Standard composite.\\n- **Orientation accuracy (OA)** — Fraction of recovered edges whose orientation matches the truth. Restricted to true edges that were recovered at all. Random-orientation baseline = 0.5.\\n\\n### 5.2 Shell recovery (F1_shell) (per U7)\\n\\n`F1_shell` is computed as follows:\\n\\n1. From the returned `S_lat[i, j] ∈ [0, 1]`, build a binary \\\"shell-edge\\\" adjacency: `A[i, j] = 1` iff `S_lat[i, j] ≥ τ`, with `τ = 0.5` as the design default and sensitivity at `τ ∈ {0.3, 0.7}`.\\n2. Cluster the resulting \\\"shall-edge\\\" graph with single-linkage clustering to obtain shell membership sets (Assumed operationalisation; thresholding alternative is a downstream substitution).\\n3. For each true latent `U_ℓ` with observed-children set `Ψ_ℓ^true`, find the best-matching recovered cluster `Ψ_ℓ^rec = argmax_C |Ψ_ℓ^true ∩ C|` (1-to-1 matching via Hungarian over precision/recall pairs).\\n4. `F1_shell` = per-shell `2·P·R/(P+R)` averaged over true latents. If no recovered cluster matches a true shell, that shell contributes `0`.\\n\\nReported as mean ± bootstrap 95% CI over replicates.\\n\\n### 5.3 Confounding-Spurious-Edge Rate (CSR) (per B)\\n\\n**Step 1 — banned-edges set.** For the true latent-augmented DAG `𝒢^*`, compute all observable pairs `(i, j)` such that adding a directed edge `X_i → X_j` (in either direction) to the observable projection of `𝒢^*` would *not* be implied by any member of `𝒢^*`'s Markov equivalence class — i.e., pairs separated in conditional independence terms via d-separation patterns that a correctly-calibrated algorithm could detect. This banned-edges set is precomputed per `(p, d, k_lat, m, topology, U_topology, ξ)` and held fixed across methods.\\n\\n**Step 2 — extra edges.** Let `E_extra(method)` be the set of directed observable edges returned by the method on a replicate that are not in the truth's equivalence-class-implied edge set.\\n\\n**Step 3 — CSR.** For each replicate, let `L_same(i,j) = 1` iff `∃` a true latent `U_ℓ` such that `U_ℓ → X_i` AND `U_ℓ → X_j` in `𝒢^*`. Then:\\n\\n```\\nCSR = |{ e = (i,j) ∈ E_extra : L_same(i,j) = 1 }| / |E_extra|\\n```\\n\\n`CSR = 0` ⇒ no spurious edges are between same-latent children (ideal); `CSR = 1` ⇒ every spurious edge is between same-latent children (latent-confounding failure mode fully explains the extras). For degenerate `|E_extra| = 0`, the cell is omitted.\\n\\nReported as: **(i) fraction `CSR`**; **(ii) absolute count `|E_extra|` per replicate**; **(iii) rate-per-shell `CSR / |true shelles|`** to normalise by latent density.\\n\\n### 5.4 Metrics logging schema (per replicate)\\n\\n`{ method, cell_id, graph_id, mech_id, lambda, k_max, tau, F1_adj, F1_skel, SHD, OA, F1_shell, |E_extra|, CSR, CSR_per_shell }`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 6. Ablation Axes & Priority Order\\n\\nShown is the order in which cells would be computed and reported if compute is staged.\\n\\n| Priority | Axis | Range | Role |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Latent strength `ξ` | `{0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0}` | The mandatory exercise of the latent-confounding claim. Evidential axis for the C-Dir claim. |\\n| 2 | #Latents `k_lat` | `{0, 1, 2, 3}` (evidential: `{1, 2, 3}`) | Connection-strength: high `k_lat` increases the magnitude and overlap of latent influence. |\\n| 3 | Sample size `n` | `{500, 2000, 10000}` | Finite-sample convergence of F1_shell; intersects priority 1 for the `F1_shell vs n` curve. |\\n| 4 | Mechanism class | `{linear-Gauss, nonlinear-additive, heterosk.}` | Where CAMUV's premise applies. Stricter cap on generative freedom. |\\n| 5 | Interventional dose `|Targets|` | `{0, 3, 6}` | Whether interventions recover latent identifiability, per U2. |\\n| 6 | Observable scale `p` | `{8, 15, 30}` | Headline at `p = 15`; `p = 8` is the small-envelope fallback (priority reflects compute cost). |\\n| 7 | Latent fan-out `m_ℓ` | `{2, 3, 4}` | Where shell membership has enough signal to recover. |\\n| 8 | Graph density `d` | `{1.0, 1.5, 3.0}` | Robustness to departures from sparsity. |\\n\\n`d = 1.5`, `m_ℓ` chosen uniformly, `p = 15` (headline), `n = 2000` (default), `|Targets| = 3` (headline), nonlinear-additive (C-Shell-relevant), `k_lat = 2` (default for headline) are the cell-fixing defaults unless an axis is itself the variable. Cells outside the small envelope are skipped under stdlib-only fallback (§10).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 7. Minimum Evidence Bar (per A)\\n\\nThe C-Dir claim is answered *positively within the small envelope* if and only if, **across the cell band**\\n\\n```\\n(p = 8, n ∈ {500, 2000}, k_lat = 2, mechanism = nonlinear-additive, ξ = 1.0)\\n```\\n\\n**both** of the following hold:\\n\\n1. **Adjacency F1 margin.** Mean `F1_adj(CAMUV) − F1_adj(FCI) ≥ +0.10` (absolute), averaged across `n ∈ {500, 2000}`. The matched-sample baseline is FCI at the same `n`, same `p`, same `k_lat`, same `ξ`, same `mechanism`. Margin is `+0.10` — the design's illustrative choice; can be revised at analysis time but is pre-committed here so the decision rule is not post-hoc tuned.\\n2. **CSR margin.** Mean `CSR(CAMUV) − CSR(FCI) ≤ −0.10` (absolute; equivalently, `CSR(FCI) − CSR(CAMUV) ≥ +0.10`).\\n\\nFailure modes for the binary decision:\\n\\n- **Negative answer** if either margin is not met over the band. In that case the design reports the smaller margin, the cause attribution from §9, and degrades the headline claim accordingly.\\n- **Inconclusive** if the margin is met but the per-axis confidence intervals overlap zero (e.g., due to NC cells or `|E_extra| = 0` omissions); the design reports this as inconclusive rather than over-claiming.\\n\\n**The minimum bar is over the small envelope only; the full envelope strengthens or weakens the answer but does not on its own contradict a positive minimum-bar verdict.**\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 8. Headline Result Figure (per C)\\n\\nThree panels, fixed conditions for panels (i) and (ii), single-method for panel (iii). Everything else in the analysis plan is reported as supporting tables.\\n\\n| Panel | x-axis | y-axis | Methods | Fixed conditions |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| (i) **F1_adj vs ξ** | Latent strength `ξ ∈ {0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0}` | `F1_adj` (mean ± 95% bootstrap CI) | `{PC, FCI, GES, CAMUV −latent, CAMUV}` | `p = 15`, `k_lat = 2`, `n = 2000`, `mechanism = nonlinear-additive`, `|Targets| = 3`, `λ` and `k_max` locked to median-best for CAMUV from §2.3 |\\n| (ii) **CSR vs ξ** | Latent strength `ξ ∈ {0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5, 2.0}` | `CSR` (mean ± 95% CI) | `{PC, FCI, GES, CAMUV −latent, CAMUV}` | Same as (i) |\\n| (iii) **F1_shell vs n** | Sample size `n ∈ {500, 2000, 10000}` | `F1_shell` (mean ± band over `τ`) | **CAMUV only**, with a ribbon `τ ∈ {0.3, 0.5, 0.7}` showing sensitivity | `p = 15`, `k_lat = 2`, `ξ = 1.0`, `mechanism = nonlinear-additive`, `|Targets| = 3` |\\n\\n**Reading rule.** Panel (i) tests whether CAMUV's F1_adj advantage grows with `ξ`; panel (ii) tests whether CAMUV's CSR *decreases* with `ξ` relative to other methods — together they indicate that the latent component is doing the work, not the no-latent baseline; panel (iii) tests whether shell recovery scales with sample size at non-trivial `ξ`, with `τ` sensitivity bounds stating how durable the shell score is.\\n\\n**Secondary figures** (not the headline): all five metrics by method over a heatmap of `(p, ξ)`; orientation accuracy vs `n` and `ξ`; CSR vs `|Targets|` (interventional dose).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 9. Error-Type Diagnosis Plan (per E)\\n\\nFor each method, build a 2×2 confusion of **recovered edges** classified as:\\n\\n| | Edge intersects true skeleton | Edge is in recovered set but not in true skeleton (extra) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Shares a true latent with its (counterfactual) true neighbor** | `TP-latent` (true edge where direction touches a latent) | `LATENT-FP` (extra edge plausibly induced by a latent) |\\n| **Does not share a latent with the (counterfactual) true neighbor** | `TP-nonlatent` | `OTHER-FP` (extra edge plausibly a different failure mode) |\\n\\nThe \\\"counterfactual true neighbor\\\" for an extra edge `(i, j)` is: any true edge `(i, ·)` or `(·, j)` that the algorithm may have been trying to discover. We use union over true neighbours of `i` and `j` so the classification is monotone.\\n\\n**Reading rule.**\\n\\n- For PC and GES, expect `LATENT-FP` to dominate under `ξ > 0`: this is the *\\\"latent confounding produced a spurious edge between two non-adjacent true neighbours\\\"* failure mode and confirms the design's worry.\\n- For CAMUV, expect `LATENT-FP` to remain low while `TP-latent` increases with `ξ`. If `LATENT-FP` is also high for CAMUV, the in-family ablation (`CAMUV −latent` vs CAMUV) is the diagnostic and the design re-attributes the gap to a non-latent component.\\n- For FCI, the diagnosis profile is method-specific and reported as such. (FCI routinely emits bidirected edges `↔` for latent nodes; `OA` will reflect this; we keep `OA`'s definition \\\"if recovered edge has any in/out orientation consistent with row truth, count it\\\", tuned for the FCI comparison.)\\n\\nThe 2×2 is reported per cell as a fraction (so it sums to 1 across `TP-latent + TP-nonlatent + LATENT-FP + OTHER-FP`). This is the primary attribution tool for \\\"true recovery vs. spurious edges\\\".\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 10. Compute Envelope & Staged Execution (per D)\\n\\n### 10.1 Small envelope — stdlib-only fallback (docs confirm no numpy / scipy / networkx are installed; pip install is blocked by bubblewrap)\\n\\n- `p ≤ 8`, `n ∈ {500, 2000}`, `k_lat ≤ 2`, ≤30 replicates per cell.\\n- The minimum-evidence bar (§7) is defined over this envelope so it can be evaluated under the documented sandbox.\\n- All metrics reduce to stdlib `random`, `math`, `statistics`, `itertools`. No `numpy`, no `scipy`, no `networkx`. Cluster connectivity, d-separation checks, and SCM sampling written in pure Python.\\n\\n### 10.2 Full envelope — assumed-dependency scope\\n\\n- `p ∈ {15, 30}`, `n ∈ {500, 2000, 10000}`, `k_lat ≤ 3`, ≤900 replicates per cell (30 × 30).\\n- Assumes the environment can supply `numpy`, `scipy`, and (effectively) a callable implementing §2.1. This is **not** the current sandbox state; the design documents it as the *intended* scope for any larger compute envelope.\\n- The headline figure (§8) is defined exclusively at `p = 15`, `n ∈ {500, 2000, 10000}`, which is reachable in either envelope for the F1_adj-vs-ξ axis; the F1_shell-vs-n axis requires `n = 10000`, which is in the small envelope's coverage.\\n\\n### 10.3 Staged execution (priority order under §6)\\n\\n1. **(small envelope)** *(p = 8, k_lat = 2, nonlinear-additive)* across the full `n × ξ × |Targets|` grid → establishes minimum-evidence bar.\\n2. **(headline subset)** *(p = 15, k_lat = 2, n = 2000, nonlinear-additive, |Targets| = 3)* across `ξ` — supplies panels (i) and (ii) of the headline figure.\\n3. **(shell scaling panel)** *(p = 15, k_lat = 2, ξ = 1.0, nonlinear-additive, |Targets| = 3)* across `n` — supplies panel (iii).\\n4. **(full envelope)** remaining axes from §6 priority 5–8.\\n\\n### 10.4 Wall-time breakdown (high-level; sized to the sandbox)\\n\\n- Per-replicate SCM draw and observable-projection computation: O((n + edges) · p) in either envelope.\\n- CI test for FCI / CAMUV: O(p² · conditioning_sets).\\n- 30 × 30 × cells × methods: scaling is linear in axes count; full envelope is the cost driver.\\n- Under stdlib-only, expected wall-time per cell rises roughly 10–50× because every vector op is Python-list-bound. The small envelope is sized to fit.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 11. Identifiability Boundaries & Negative Controls (per U8, F)\\n\\nThis section documents, pre-experiment, where CAMUV's claim is bounded.\\n\\n### 11.1 Negative-control cells (per U8)\\n\\n| Cell | Construction | Invariant Tested | Disposition |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| **NC-OL** | `k_lat = 2` latents, both with `|Ψ_U ∩ V| ≥ 2` shared observed children. | Whether shell F1 can be defined given ambiguous latent identities. | Reported in a separate table labelled \\\"unidentifiable / not evidential\\\"; excluded from headline. |\\n| **NC-FM** | Single latent `L → B`; `A → L → B`; `B` the only observed child. | Whether shell with `|Ψ_U|=1` can be detected at all. | Reported in the same negative-control table. The expected behaviour is `F1_shell = 0` for all methods; this is a fixture-check, not an evidential test. |\\n\\n### 11.2 What the design pre-commits to ignoring (from §1's do-NOT list, restated for traceability)\\n\\n- Full latent-graph F1.\\n- Identifiability beyond Markov / projection equivalence.\\n- Behaviour outside the swept `λ × k_max` box.\\n- Mechanisms outside `{linear-Gauss, nonlinear-additive, heterosk.}`.\\n- Resolution of NC-FM-style fully-mediated latents.\\n- Runtime and scaling outside §10.\\n\\n### 11.3 Mid-design identifiability sanity check\\n\\nFor each cell, we annotate whether the configured cell is \\\"identifiably split\\\" from its Markov-equivalent class given the observables. If it is not, the cell is moved from \\\"evidential\\\" to \\\"ambiguous\\\" in the results table and is excluded from the headline. The design reports the proportion of cells moved under this check so the headline's robustness can be evaluated.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 12. Open Clarifications Deferred to a Future Round\\n\\nA short list. None of these block the design; each is a downstream substitution decision.\\n\\n1. **Metric tolerance for FCI `↔` edges (C-Dir contrast)** — The design's \\\"any matching orientation counts\\\" rule is conservative; an exact-direction comparison can be added as a secondary column if needed.\\n2. **Clustering operator for shells** — Single-linkage is the design default; alternative operators (e.g., thresholded-similarity) become a future-round sensitivity axis if empirical `F1_shell` is sensitive.\\n3. **`λ` and `k_max` defaulting when implementation flags do not match** — The design's fall-back is lock at `λ = 1e-2` and `k_max = 3`; a future round may supply adjusted sweeps.\\n4. **CAMUV's exact output projection semantics (CPDAG vs PAG vs MAG)** — The design scores against an \\\"as-if CPDAG on observables\\\" with tolerance for bidirected/marked edges. A future round can substitute a stricter projector.\\n5. **Stability of `τ = 0.5` for shell threshold** — Sensitivity at `{0.3, 0.7}` is in §5; a future round may introduce a calibration set if `S_lat` is uncalibrated.\\n6. **Whether realizations of CAMUV ignore `I`** — The escape clause in §2.2 records but does not auto-correct.\\n\\n---\\n\\n*End of design. No experiment was executed; no empirical numbers appear; all CAMUV-internal specifications are Assumed per §2.1 and per the inspection report (no CAMUV material is present in `/workspace`).*\",\"evidence\":\"Inspection of /workspace (verified live):\\n- `ls -la /workspace` returned only `task.json` (401 bytes). No code, no docs, no datasets.\\n- `cat /workspace/task.json` showed the question, context, and quoted `track.md` text inside the JSON — no actual `track.md` file.\\n- `find / -maxdepth {3,4} -iname \\\"*camuv*\\\"` and `-iname \\\"*causal*\\\"` returned nothing in non-system locations.\\n- `ls -la /home/actor` and `/tmp` empty.\\n- Python 3.12.3 is present but `numpy`, `scipy`, `sklearn`, `networkx`, `pandas`, `matplotlib` all raise `ModuleNotFoundError`. Only stdlib and a small set of system packages (boto3, cryptography, etc.) are present.\\n- Network is disabled per task config; `pip install` blocked.\\n\\nAll binding decisions above are sourced from the researcher's approval message (resolutions to U1–U8 plus additions A–F). The eight resolutions and six additions are recorded verbatim or with explicit paraphrase; the document does not introduce new claims about CAMUV's internals. The minimum evidence bar is `+0.10 / +0.10` as the design's illustrative threshold per A. The headline figure's three panels are fixed exactly per C at (p=15, k_lat=2, n=2000, nonlinear-additive, |Targets|=3, λ and k_max median-best) for panels (i) and (ii), and (p=15, k_lat=2, ξ=1.0, nonlinear-additive, |Targets|=3) with τ∈{0.3,0.5,0.7} band for panel (iii). Compute is staged per D with stdlib-only small envelope for the feasibility fallback.\",\"assumptions\":\"Document-level assumption set (all binding to the design; restated compactly):\\n\\n(A) **About CAMUV internals — all Assumed; no /workspace material grounds them.** (i) CAMUV returns an augmented CPDAG on observed variables with per-pair latent-shell scores; (ii) it consumes a per-row intervention indicator; (iii) primary hyperparameters are λ (regularisation) and a latent-cardinality cap k_max; (iv) the latent-term contribution is flaggable for an in-family ablation, with fallback to a no-latent counterpart sharing other hyperparameters; (v) the produced S_lat is a per-pair score in [0,1] interpretable as a shell-membership proxy via single-linkage at threshold τ.\\n\\n(B) **About SCMs / identifiability — Assumed from general causal-discovery knowledge.** Observables DAG defined up to Markov equivalence; shell = observed children of a latent; linear-Gaussian latent equivalence class is broad; interventions increase identifiability proportionately to |Targets|; mechanism-class mismatch is reported separately from accuracy.\\n\\n(C) **About the sandbox — Repo's claims (sandbox= bubblewrap; network off; system python3 only; no scientific stack; /workspace contains only task.json; no CAMUV code, docs, or datasets anywhere up to depth 4; no track.md file as a separate input — its only presence is the quoted string inside task.json.documents.track.md). Compute feasibility (small envelope p≤8, n≤2000 within stdlib-only; full envelope requires numpy/scipy) is treated as a documented branch, not a numeric claim.\\n\\n(D) **About the design choices.** Swept hyperparameter box λ∈{1e−3, 1e−2, 1e−1} and k_max∈{1,2,3}; τ=0.5 default with sensitivity at {0.3, 0.7}; minimum bar thresholds ±0.10 absolute (illustrative); k_lat=0 is the no-latent control baseline (not an evidential cell for CAMUV's claim); p=15 is the headline envelope with p=8 the small-envelope fallback; the headline figure uses nonlinear-additive at (p=15, k_lat=2, n=2000, |Targets|=3) for panels (i) and (ii), with λ and k_max locked to median-best.\\n\\n(E) **Items intentionally left open.** Single-linkage is the default shell-clustering operator; FCI's `↔` edges are scored under a \\\"any matching orientation counts\\\" tolerance; CAMUV's exact CPDAG-vs-PAG-vs-MAG output projection is taken as \\\"as-if CPDAG on observables\\\" with tolerance; implementation-flag absences fall back to locked defaults (λ=1e-2, k_max=3). These are the §12 deferred items, not silently absorbed into the design.\",\"uncertainties\":\"Items §12 of the design (restated):\\n\\n1. Whether FCI `↔` edges should be scored under a stricter directionality rule (design default: lenient; secondary column if requested).\\n2. Whether single-linkage is the best shell-clustering operator (design default; downstream sensitivity).\\n3. The exact hyperparameter defaults when implementation flags do not match the design's API (fall-back: lock at λ=1e-2, k_max=3).\\n4. CAMUV's true output projection semantics (CPDAG vs PAG vs MAG); scored under an \\\"as-if CPDAG\\\" with tolerance.\\n5. Whether τ=0.5 needs calibration per replicate.\\n6. Whether the realised CAMUV callable ignores the per-row intervention indicator I (escape clause documented; not auto-corrected).\\n\\nBeyond §12: the minimum-bar threshold of ±0.10 absolute is treated as illustrative — both as a sensitivity value (a future round may revise) and as a design choice (so the decision rule is not post-hoc tuned). Identifiability mid-design check (§11.3) may move some cells out of \\\"evidential\\\" into \\\"ambiguous\\\"; the design reports the proportion of such moves rather than absorbing them.\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-d369f59a-e9ad-407a-998e-a337c497c7ee", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 21}
